      </div><div class="container">
      <h2>The Key Difference</h2>
      <p>A passive dead hang relaxes the shoulders, and an active hang pulls the shoulder blades down and back. Both start with an overhand grip on a pull-up bar and both keep the arms straight. The difference lives in your shoulder blades.</p>
      <p>Passive dead hangs keep the shoulders relaxed. Your shoulder blades slide upward and your shoulders rise toward your ears. Your bodyweight pulls down through the arms without muscular resistance at the shoulder.</p>
      <p>Active hangs engage the shoulder blades. You pull them down and back while keeping your arms straight. Your shoulders drop a few centimeters away from your ears, and your lats and lower trapezius work. The bar stays in the same place but the demand shifts from stretch to stability.</p>
      <p>Both versions share the same <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">overhand grip setup</a>, and in both the fingers support your full bodyweight. The shoulder position changes which tissues get the main training stimulus.</p>

      <h2>Passive Dead Hang</h2>
      <p>Grip the bar at shoulder width, step off and let your shoulders rise toward your ears. Relax every muscle except your forearms. Breathe slowly and let gravity do the work.</p>
      <p>In any straight-arm hang the fingers support your bodyweight. The flexor digitorum profundus and superficialis keep your fingers closed around the bar. Passive hangs let you hold longer than active hangs because the shoulder muscles work less.</p>
      <p>Your spine is unloaded while you hang, because gravity pulls the pelvis away from the ribcage. Its effect on discs has not been measured in the sources cited on this site. See the <a href="/spine-decompression/">spine decompression guide</a> for the evidence.</p>
      <p>The lats, pecs and teres major stretch through their overhead range, and the upper back extends slightly as the ribcage opens. Many people use passive hangs as a stretch after sitting.</p>

      <h2>Active Dead Hang</h2>
      <p>Grip the bar as in a passive hang, then pull your shoulder blades down and slightly together without bending your elbows. Your body rises a few centimeters as the shoulder blades move down. Hold this position with straight arms.</p>
      <p>The lower trapezius and serratus anterior do much of the work. These muscles control shoulder blade depression and upward rotation. Weakness here is associated with shoulder problems and difficulty with pull-ups.</p>
      <p>An active hang practices the shoulder blade position used at the start of a pull-up. Pull-ups, overhead presses and handstands also depend on stable shoulder blades under load.</p>
      <p>Hold each active hang for 15 to 20 seconds. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription. The muscular demand is higher, so your times will be shorter than passive hangs. Keep the shoulder blades in position, because upward drift means you have lost the active component. Read the <a href="/shoulder-health/">shoulder health guide</a> for more on shoulder programming.</p>

      <h2>Passive vs Active Comparison Table</h2>
      <p>The table compares what each hang loads and what each suits.</p>
