      <h2>When to Use Passive Hangs</h2>
      <p>Use passive dead hangs when your goal is a relaxed stretch or grip endurance. Many people use them as a cooldown after heavy lifting or a long period of sitting.</p>
      <p>Some people find short passive hangs ease stiffness in the back and shoulders. Holds of 10 to 30 seconds are common training practice, not a tested prescription. Ask a clinician before hanging with back pain or a diagnosed spine condition.</p>
      <p>Passive hangs double as a lat and chest stretch. A <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorway pull-up bar</a> makes hanging easy to fit into the day.</p>
      <p>Grip sessions suit passive hangs because the shoulder muscles work less, so your fingers become the limit. In any straight-arm hang the fingers support your bodyweight.</p>

      <h2>When to Use Active Hangs</h2>
      <p>Use active hangs when you want shoulder blade control. An active hang can open an upper body session, and a passive hang can follow it.</p>
      <p>Active hangs practice the shoulder blade movement that starts a pull-up. Building to about 20 seconds of controlled shoulder blade depression before you attempt full pull-ups is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
      <p>Some climbers use active hangs as part of a warm-up. No study shows that this reduces injury.</p>
      <p>Some physiotherapists use active hangs in shoulder rehab. Ask a clinician before using them for pain, and stop if symptoms increase. Skip passive hangs, or ask a clinician first, if you have a history of shoulder dislocation or instability.</p>

      <h2>How to Program Both</h2>
      <p>In a session made only of hangs, start with passive hangs and finish with active hangs. Before an upper body session, an active hang can open the workout and a passive hang can follow it.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>Sample Combined Protocol</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Passive hangs:</strong> 3 sets of 20 to 30 seconds. Rest 60 seconds between sets.</li>
          <li><strong>Active hangs:</strong> 3 sets of 15 to 20 seconds. Rest 60 seconds between sets.</li>
          <li><strong>Frequency:</strong> Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days. Add a session only if you stay pain free.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>The sample protocol is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
      </div>

      <p>If time is limited, alternate emphasis between sessions. One session leads with passive hangs and one set of active hangs, and the next leads with active hangs and one set of passive hangs. Track your hold times for each variation separately, because the two hangs are different tests.</p>
      <p>Follow the <a href="/deadhang-progressions/">dead hang progression ladder</a> to advance both variations step by step.</p>

      <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>
      <h3>Doing Active When Intending Passive</h3>
      <p>Many beginners do not fully relax during passive hangs. Their shoulders stay partly engaged out of habit or fear, which gives a hybrid position that is neither a full passive stretch nor a clear active hang.</p>
      <p>Fix this by shrugging your shoulders toward your ears after gripping the bar. Let your neck disappear. Your arms should feel like ropes connecting your hands to your torso.</p>

      <h3>Not Engaging Fully During Active Hangs</h3>
      <p>The opposite mistake happens during active hangs. Partial shoulder blade depression looks correct from the outside but recruits the lower trapezius less. Your body rises only slightly instead of a few centimeters.</p>
      <p>Fix this by imagining you push the bar away from your body while pulling your shoulder blades toward your back pockets. The contraction should feel clear across the mid-back.</p>

      <h3>Bending the Elbows</h3>
      <p>Elbow bend turns an active hang into a partial pull-up. Keep your arms straight in both variations so the shoulder joint and forearms do the work without biceps assistance.</p>

      <h3>Holding Your Breath</h3>
      <p>Breathe continuously during both variations. Holding your breath briefly raises blood pressure and shortens many holds.</p>
      <p>Review <a href="/deadhang-form/">form and breathing technique</a> for complete guidance.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>What is the difference between a dead hang and an active hang?</h3>
        <p>A dead hang keeps your shoulders relaxed so they rise toward your ears. An active hang pulls the shoulder blades down and back. Both use the same overhand bar grip and in both the fingers support your bodyweight, but the shoulder muscles work more in an active hang.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Should I do passive or active hangs first?</h3>
        <p>In a session made only of hangs, start with passive hangs to stretch the shoulders and finish with active hangs to practice shoulder blade control while the joints are warm. Before an upper body workout, an active hang can open the session and a passive hang can follow it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Can I do both passive and active hangs in the same workout?</h3>
        <p>Yes. A common plan is 2 to 3 sets of passive hangs for 20 to 30 seconds each, then 2 to 3 sets of active hangs for 15 to 20 seconds each, with 60 seconds of rest between sets. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
      </div>

